<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahauddin Zakariya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1170–<a href="/years/1262">1262</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Baha-ud-din Zakariya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahauddin Zakariya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, who brought the Suhrawardi order to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. A disciple of Shihab al-Din Umar al-Suhrawardi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, he settled at Multan and made it a major centre of Sufism, keeping a large hospice. His tomb in Multan is a pilgrimage site, and Multan's university is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahauddin-zakariya.jpg" alt="Bahauddin Zakariya" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balochlens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_view_of_Bahauddin_Zakariya_Tomb_by_Balochlens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
